// on Today, Binko shares a fun fact about Cebu; a new one each time you come back to the tab
// Cebu and Philippines fun facts ({t, ph:1} = Philippines-wide), each told by the Lucky Clover it suits best, in their own voice. Split evenly so nobody shows up more than the others.
const CLOVER_FACTS = {"green": ["In 1565, a Spanish soldier named Juan de Camuz found the Santo Niño statue, an image of the Child Jesus, inside a burned house. It had first come to Cebu about 44 years before. A GOOD finding! I will thank Mother Rhine for it, right after lunch, HAHA!", "'Sutukil' is a Cebu seafood style. It joins three words: sugba (grilled), tuwa (soup) and kilaw (fresh fish soaked in vinegar). Three ways to eat one fish? Triton can have the bones, BWAHAHA, I want all three NOW!", "The Simala Shrine in Sibonga is a church that looks like a fairy-tale castle. A group of monks who came from Pampanga started it in 1998. A castle for praying! I would pray to Mother Rhine there while Quickare tips his top hat at the door.", "Rosquillos are ring-shaped cookies with wavy edges. Margarita 'Titay' Frasco first made them in Liloan in 1907, and people say President Sergio Osmeña named them. Good name, BETTER cookie! I could eat a whole box before Ollie even sees it.", "Lechon is a whole roasted pig, and it is Cebu's most famous dish. Cebu-style lechon is stuffed with herbs like lemongrass, so many people eat it with no sauce at all. No sauce means no waiting, even better! My stomach rumbles so loud that Doffy is barking back.", "Bantayan Island in northern Cebu has a local dish called taguangkan. It is grilled chicken ovary, with tiny eggs still inside. I am NOT picky, I would try it today! Binko would probably want to study it first, GAHAHA!", "Anthony Bourdain, the American chef and travel-TV host, visited Cebu for his show No Reservations. He called the lechon, a whole roasted pig, the 'best pig ever.' I have not tried it, and I agree ANYWAY. HAHA, now I am hungry again!", "In Oslob, a town in southern Cebu, visitors can swim near whale sharks, the biggest fish in the ocean. Despite the name, they eat tiny plankton, not people. I respect a big eater! Ollie says not to swim with them on an empty stomach, BWAHAHA!", "In 2025, the Michelin Guide, a world-famous restaurant rating book, judged the Philippines for the first time. Six Cebu eateries won a 'Bib Gourmand' for great food at fair prices, including Pares Batchoy Food House. I will visit all six in ONE day! Doffy can carry me home.", "In 2019, the Netflix show Street Food: Asia filmed an episode in Cebu City. One star was Tatay Entoy of Cordova, who cooks bakasi, a reef eel, fried or in a sour stew. I have eaten eel before, but I want HIS eel, two bowls. HOHOHA!", "Outside the Basilica of the Santo Niño in Cebu City, older women sell candles. If you ask, they pray for you while dancing sinulog steps, and many learned the job from their mothers. A prayer you can dance! Mother Rhine would love it, and Triton could sing along.", "Torta from Argao, a town in southern Cebu, is a soft cake made with pork lard, egg yolks and tuba, a coconut wine, used instead of yeast. It is baked in a clay oven called a hudnohan. Lard in a cake? I trust this cake completely, HAHA!", {"t": "In 2024, TasteAtlas, a popular online food guide, ranked the top 100 Filipino dishes. Number one was chicken inasal, grilled chicken from Bacolod in the Visayas, and number two was lechon, a whole roasted pig. Why choose? I want BOTH on one plate, right now!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Lolong was a saltwater crocodile caught in Bunawan, a town in the southern Philippines. Guinness World Records named him the largest crocodile in captivity: 6.17 meters long and more than a ton. My big cousin! I am only 7 foot 6, so he wins, BWAHAHA!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In 2023, Iloilo City, on Panay Island in the Visayas, became the first place in the Philippines named a UNESCO Creative City of Gastronomy. Gastronomy means the art of good food. A whole city honored for cooking! Pack the wagon, Triton, we are going.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Balbacua is a Visayan stew of oxtail, beef skin and knuckles, simmered four to six hours until the broth turns thick, almost like jelly. Its name likely comes from the Spanish 'barbacoa,' a cousin of the word 'barbecue.' Six hours is TOO long, but I will wait, barely, HAHA!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In 2023, the Philippine men's basketball team, Gilas Pilipinas, beat Jordan 70 to 60 to win gold at the Asian Games. It was their first Asian Games basketball gold since 1962. Sixty-one years of waiting! I know that feeling, it is how long lunch takes.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "The Philippine eagle is the national bird and one of the largest eagles in the world, and a pair raises only about one chick every two years. It was once called the 'monkey-eating eagle,' but it also hunts snakes, lizards and hornbills. A big eater with a varied menu, just like me! HAHA!", "ph": 1}, "Bantayan Island, off the northern tip of Cebu, is known as the 'egg basket' of the Visayas, because its farms produce about 2 million eggs a day! Two million eggs? That is breakfast for me and Ollie, and maybe Triton gets one, BWAHAHA!", "Off Malapascua Island in northern Cebu, divers can see thresher sharks, which have very long, whip-like tails. My tail is long too, but theirs is fancier, HAHA! Quickare would want one to match his coat.", "Near Moalboal, a town on Cebu's west coast, millions of sardines swim in a giant silvery swirl just off the beach. MILLIONS of sardines, and I am supposed to just watch? Binko, hold me back!", {"t": "The Philippine tarsier is a tiny primate with giant eyes. Each eye is about as big as its whole brain! My one prosthetic eye sees in Truedark, and even I am impressed. HAHA, Binko should meet this little guy.", "ph": 1}, "Cebu Safari and Adventure Park in Carmen, a town in northern Cebu, opened in 2018. It has about 1,000 animals from around 120 species, plus a zipline about 1.3 kilometers long. A thousand animals, and I promise to eat NONE of them. Ollie, write that down, HAHA!", "The Camotes Islands, east of Cebu, are named after sweet potatoes! In 1582, a Spanish writer noted that the islanders grew a root they called 'camotes.' Islands named after a snack? Best kind of island, and now I am hungry.", "Cebu Ocean Park opened in Cebu City in 2019. It calls itself the largest oceanarium in the Philippines, with sharks, rays, jellyfish, penguins and sea lion shows. I would go just for the sea lions, and then straight to lunch. Triton can sing to the sharks, BWAHAHA!", {"t": "Ube is a purple yam from the Philippines, used in treats like ube halaya, a sweet purple jam. By 2026 it was so trendy around the world that news writers asked if it was the next matcha. Purple snacks? I want ALL of them, and Ollie can have the pink ones, HAHA!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Bohol, an island in the Visayas, has as many as 1,776 round hills that turn brown in the dry season, so people call them the Chocolate Hills. One legend says a heartbroken giant wept, and the hills grew where his tears fell. Chocolate Hills that are not chocolate? That is cruel to a hungry crocodile.", "ph": 1}, "Cebu kids play 'pasabong ug damang,' or spider fighting! They keep spiders in matchboxes, set two on a stick and nudge them together. Playing is legal, but betting is illegal gambling, and in September 2024 police in Cebu City arrested 46 people at spider fights. Over SPIDERS! HAHA, Binko would bet his mech on the fuzzy one.", {"t": "Talunan means 'loser' in Tagalog. It is a sour stew of vinegar, garlic and ginger, traditionally cooked from the tough meat of roosters that lost a cockfight. Lose the fight, end up in the pot! Tough meat never scared me, I would still ask for seconds, BWAHAHA!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Balut is a boiled duck egg with a developing chick inside, usually incubated 14 to 18 days, then eaten with salt or chili-garlic vinegar. It is said that Chinese traders brought the idea to the Philippines around 1565. Ollie looks away when I crack one open. I eat THREE, HAHA!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Dinuguan is pork offal stewed in pig's blood and vinegar, and its name comes from 'dugo,' the word for blood. In Cebuano it is also called 'dugo-dugo,' and some people call it 'chocolate meat.' It is NOT chocolate, which is a crime, but with puto rice cakes I forgive it. Doffy, that is MY bowl!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "At a Philippine cockfight, bets are taken by a 'kristo,' named for his outstretched hands. A referee called a 'sentensyador' decides the winner, and his ruling is final, no appeals. I like a man who decides fast! I would make a fine kristo too, my arms are LONG, BWAHAHA!", "ph": 1}, {"t": "People in the Visayas tell of the manananggal, a creature that splits at the waist and flies off on bat wings, leaving its legs behind! Legend says it hunts sleeping pregnant women with a long tongue, and salt, garlic or ash on the leftover half keeps it from rejoining before sunrise. Half a monster? Only half a snack, BWAHAHA!", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "According to a Visayan legend, Bathala made seven moons, and a giant sea serpent called the bakunawa swallowed six! During eclipses, people beat drums and made loud noises to scare it off the last one. A snake that eats MOONS? Even I am not that hungry. Ollie, grab a drum, HAHA!", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Legend says Maria Cacao, the goddess of Mount Lantoy in Argao, Cebu, lives in a cave with cacao growing outside. When the river floods or a bridge breaks, people say she and her husband Mangao are sailing their golden ship to sell their crops abroad. A CHOCOLATE goddess! Mother Rhine, forgive me, I must visit her.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "The kapre is a hairy tree giant of Filipino folklore, said to sit in big trees like balete and mango, smoking a huge cigar. People say fireflies in the woods are sparks from its cigar, and that it likes to lead travelers in circles. Sitting in a tree all day? Triton, that is my dream job, HAHA!", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "In northern Luzon legend, the berberoka is a swamp ogre that drains a pond, leaving the fish flopping on the mud. When people come to grab them, it floods the pond again and eats them! The one thing it fears is crabs. Free fish is a TRAP? Good thing I eat crabs too, BWAHAHA!", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "The tikbalang is a tall, long-limbed creature, half man and half horse, said to confuse travelers just for fun. When it rains while the sun is shining, Filipinos say 'Kinakasal ang tikbalang,' meaning a tikbalang is getting married! A wedding means a FEAST. Doffy, follow that sun shower, HAHA!", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Manny Pacquiao, the boxer who won world titles in eight weight divisions, retired in September 2021 with these words: \"I just heard the final bell. The boxing is over.\" EIGHT divisions! I have only won one fight with lunch, and lunch fought back. Respect, champ, HAHA!", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Hidilyn Diaz, the weightlifter who won the Philippines' first-ever Olympic gold medal at the Tokyo Olympics in 2021, told reporters the next morning: \"Nothing is impossible, even in this pandemic.\" She lifted for a whole COUNTRY! I lift Doffy's food bowl and call it a workout. BWAHAHA, she is my hero now!", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Carlos Yulo, the gymnast who won two gold medals at the 2024 Paris Olympics, said days later: \"Hindi pa po ako titigil, gusto ko pa po mag-Olympics sa 2028 sa LA\" [I'm not stopping yet, I still want to go to the 2028 Olympics in LA]. Two golds and still HUNGRY? My kind of guy, HAHA!", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "A Cebuano proverb, or panultihon, says: \"Dili tanang magkatawa malipayon\" [Not all who laugh are happy]. Hmph! I laugh ALL the time, and I am happy most of it, unless lunch is late. Then I laugh louder so Ollie hurries up, BWAHAHA!", "k": "quote"}], "ollie": ["The Cebu flowerpecker is a tiny, colorful bird found only on Cebu. People thought it was extinct for most of the 1900s, until it was rediscovered in 1992.", "Lapulapu was a chieftain of Mactan Island. On April 27, 1521, he defeated the men of Ferdinand Magellan, the Portuguese explorer sailing for Spain, in the Battle of Mactan. Magellan died in that fight.", "Aguinid Falls is in Samboan, a town in southern Cebu, and it has several levels. Visitors can climb them step by step, right through the water.", "In Cebuano, a single word can carry two related meanings. 'Adlaw' means both 'sun' and 'day,' and 'bulan' means both 'moon' and 'month.'", "Pilita Corrales was a singer born in Lahug, Cebu City, in 1939. She was known as 'Asia's Queen of Songs,' and she was famous for leaning far backward while she sang.", "'Usahay' is a beloved Visayan love song. Its title comes from the Cebuano word for 'sometimes.'", "Tuslob buwa means 'dip in bubbles.' Diners take puso, which is rice in a woven coconut-leaf pouch, and dip it into a bubbling pan of rich, savory sauce.", "Monique Lhuillier is a fashion designer born in Cebu City. She makes wedding and evening gowns, which have been worn by stars like Taylor Swift and Reese Witherspoon.", "Sinulog is Cebu's biggest festival, and in 2017 the singer and actress Karencitta released 'Cebuana,' a song she made as its theme. It hit number 1 on Spotify Philippines' Viral chart, and its video got a million views on its first day.", "According to Visayan legend, Sri Lumay, Cebu's founding ruler, burned the land to drive off raiders. That scorched-earth tactic gave the place the name Sugbu, which is the root of Sugbo, Cebu's old name.", "Sirao Flower Garden sits in the mountains of Cebu City. It is nicknamed 'Little Amsterdam,' and it has rows of bright celosia flowers and a Dutch-style windmill. Photos of Sirao's flower farms went viral in 2015.", "Junior Kilat is a reggae band from Cebu City that has been playing since 2004. The band is named after Leon Kilat, a Cebu band from the 1970s, and its song 'Ako si M16' won Song of the Year at the 2005 NU Rock Awards.", "The SM Seaside Cebu Arena opened in July 2026 with a concert by BINI, a Filipino pop girl group. The arena holds up to 25,000 people, which makes it the largest indoor arena in Cebu and the Visayas.", {"t": "'Kilig' is a Filipino word for the giddy, fluttery thrill you get from something romantic. In 2016, the Oxford English Dictionary added it, so English now has a word for that feeling too.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "'Hello, Love, Again' is a 2024 romance movie starring Kathryn Bernardo and Alden Richards. It became the first Filipino film to earn over one billion pesos at the box office.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In 2025, Ahtisa Manalo from Quezon province represented the Philippines at Miss Universe, held that year in Thailand. She finished 3rd runner-up, which placed her in the top five.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In 2025, 19-year-old Filipina tennis player Alex Eala beat world number 2 Iga Swiatek at the Miami Open. She became the first Filipina ever to reach a semifinal on the WTA Tour, the top level of women's pro tennis.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "At the 2024 Paris Olympics, Carlos Yulo, a gymnast from Manila, won two gold medals, in floor exercise and vault. The Philippines' very first Olympic gold had come only three years earlier, in 2021.", "ph": 1}, "The festival name Sinulog comes from 'sulog,' the Cebuano word for a water current. The Sinulog dance looks like water flowing forward and back.", "Devotees of the Santo Niño, Cebu's holy image of the Child Jesus, shout 'Viva Pit Señor!' 'Pit' is short for 'sangpit,' a Cebuano word for calling out loudly to someone, or to God, with a request, so the cry means something like 'Hail Lord, listen to our prayers!'", "A native Cebuano speaker can proudly call themself 'Bisdak.' The word blends 'Bisaya' and 'dako,' from the phrase 'Bisayang dako.'", "The Cebu boobook is a small owl of Cebu's last forests. For years it was lumped in with another owl, until scientists named it its own species in 2012, mainly because its voice was different.", {"t": "The top local song on Spotify in the Philippines in 2025 was 'Multo' by Cup of Joe, a band from Baguio, and 'multo' means 'ghost.' It was also the first song by a Filipino act to enter the Billboard Global 200 chart.", "ph": 1}, "In 2024, bones found in the ceiling of a century-old house in Talisay City, Cebu, made people whisper 'sigbin,' a Visayan legend creature said to walk backward with its head between its hind legs. Veterinarians studied the photos and said the bones were most likely from a cat.", "The Cebu flowerpecker is only about 11 to 12 centimeters long. It is one of the rarest birds in the world, with only around 100 left.", "The black shama is a dark songbird with a sweet voice. It lives only on Cebu Island and nowhere else in the world.", {"t": "'Gigil' is a Filipino word for the intense feeling you get when something is so cute you want to squeeze it. In 2025, the Oxford English Dictionary added it.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Cockfighting is 'bulang' in Cebuano and 'sabong' in Tagalog. A thin, sharp blade called a 'tari' is tied to one leg of each rooster. Antonio Pigafetta, the chronicler of Magellan's voyage, recorded cockfighting in the islands in 1521, so the sport was already here before Spanish rule.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Cockfighting is legal in the Philippines under the Cockfighting Law of 1974, but only in licensed cockpits on Sundays, certain holidays and a few other occasions. Since 1948 it has been banned every December 30, Rizal Day. A 2026 report cited an estimate that the industry is worth at least 1 billion US dollars a year.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "E-sabong was online betting on livestreamed cockfights, and the Philippines was described as the only country that allowed it. In May 2022, President Rodrigo Duterte ordered it stopped after dozens of cockfighting enthusiasts went missing. In December 2022, President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. made the ban official with an executive order.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Around 2021 and 2022, about 34 'sabungeros,' or cockfighting enthusiasts, disappeared. In 2025, a whistleblower claimed they had been killed and dumped in Taal Lake, and searchers recovered sacks of bones there for forensic testing. In December 2025, the Department of Justice indicted businessman Charlie 'Atong' Ang and 21 others, and Ang's lawyer called the ruling flawed. A court issued arrest warrants in January 2026.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "The Animal Welfare Act of 1998 banned killing dogs for food in the Philippines, and the Anti-Rabies Act of 2007 raised the penalties for trading dog meat. Even so, a 2013 report by the Animal Welfare Institute estimated that about half a million dogs were still eaten each year, with Baguio as the center of the trade.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Capiz, a province on Panay Island in the Visayas, has long been teased as the home of the aswang, a shape-shifting monster. The Aswang Project, a folklore site, notes that a hereditary movement disorder called XDP is found mostly on Panay, and its twisting symptoms may once have been mistaken for transformation. People with XDP are still sometimes wrongly called aswang.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "In Visayan folklore, the wakwak is a night-flying creature with sharp claws, named for the 'wak wak' sound of its wings. The tiktik, a kind of aswang, is also known by its sound, which people say is faint when it is near and loud when it is far. So a quiet tiktik is the one to worry about.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "According to Visayan folklore, the sigbin looks like a hornless goat with huge ears it can clap like hands, and it gives off a foul smell. It is said to hunt during Holy Week and drink blood from people's shadows. Families called 'sigbinan' are said to command these creatures and keep them in clay jars.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Siquijor, an island province in the Visayas, is nicknamed the 'Island of Sorcerers.' Its most feared spell, according to local belief, is 'barang': a sorcerer keeps biting insects like bees, centipedes and beetles in a bamboo tube, then sends them to harm a target. A sorcerer who uses barang is called a mambabarang.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "In an old Visayan creation myth, a bird pecks open a floating bamboo, and the first man and woman step out. Spanish writer Miguel de Loarca recorded a version in 1582. The famous names 'Malakas' (strong) and 'Maganda' (beautiful) came much later, first appearing in a 1941 essay.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "'Pasma' is a Filipino folk illness. People believe that if hot, tired muscles suddenly meet cold, like a cold shower after hard work, you get trembling hands, sweaty palms, numbness and pain. Medical textbooks do not recognize pasma, though some of its symptoms resemble those of diabetes or thyroid problems.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "\"Ang hindi marunong lumingon sa pinanggalingan ay hindi makararating sa paroroonan\" [Those who do not look back at where they came from will never reach where they are going] is a Tagalog proverb popularly credited to Jose Rizal, the national hero. Historian Ambeth Ocampo says there is no record that Rizal ever wrote it, and a 2025 fact-check rated the attribution false.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "A famous Tagalog proverb about never forgetting where you came from is often credited to Jose Rizal, the national hero whose novels exposed abuses under Spanish rule, but he probably did not write it. He did write, on the title page of his youthful play \"El Consejo de los Dioses\": \"Con el recuerdo del pasado, entro en el porvenir\" [With the memory of the past, I enter the future].", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Manuel L. Quezon, president of the Commonwealth of the Philippines under American rule, is remembered for saying: \"I prefer a government run like hell by Filipinos to a government run like heaven by Americans.\" It dates to the early 1920s, when he was pushing for independence, and versions differ; one adds that a Filipino government, however bad, might be improved.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Benigno \"Ninoy\" Aquino Jr. was an opposition senator who was shot dead at Manila's airport in 1983 when he returned from exile. He is remembered for \"The Filipino is worth dying for,\" but in his 1980 speech to the Asia Society in New York he actually asked it as a question: \"Is the Filipino worth suffering, or even dying, for?\"", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}], "quickare": ["Welcome, friend, come sit a spell. Lapu-Lapu City on Mactan Island is famous for handmade guitars and ukuleles, and families there have been building them for generations. I do admire a craft that each family carries on in its own way.", "Make yourself comfortable while I tell you about Cebu Island's shape. It is about 196 kilometers from north to south, but only about 32 kilometers across at its widest. Long and slender, like a fine necktie laid out in the sea.", "Many Cebuano words come from Spanish, like 'lamesa' (table), 'kutsara' (spoon), 'bintana' (window) and 'silya' (chair). Pull a silya up to my lamesa, and I will fetch you a kutsara. You are always welcome at my table.", "Pungko-pungko is Cebu street food, like fried snacks and puso rice. The name comes from 'pungko,' meaning to squat or sit low, because diners sit on small low benches. For once, a dwarf like me would sit at just the right height.", "Canyoneering is an adventure of jumping, swimming and sliding down a river canyon, and it is a popular thing to do in Badian and Alegria in southwestern Cebu. Take your time on the rocks, friend, there is no hurry. I admire anyone who finds their own way down a river.", "The Cebu Taoist Temple was built in 1972 by Cebu's Chinese community. It sits 110 meters up in the hills, and its entrance looks like a piece of the Great Wall of China. As a fellow who once made his home in a hill, I admire a house that stands out on one.", "Cebu was the first capital of the Spanish colony in the Philippines, before the capital moved to Manila. That is why Cebu City is often called the oldest city in the country. An old city with its own story, and I am always glad to sit and listen to it.", "Fort San Pedro is a three-cornered Spanish fort by Cebu's harbor. It was first built of wood in 1565, and it is the oldest triangular fort of its kind in the Philippines. Three corners, in a shape all its own, and I do admire that.", "In 2025, Guinness World Records named the 'Claw King' at TOPS in Busay, Cebu City, the world's largest claw machine. It is about 5.2 meters long and 3.9 meters tall. A machine like no other, and I would gladly climb up in my top hat to try for a prize.", "IKEA, the big Swedish furniture store, is set to open its first store in Cebu and the whole Visayas in December 2026, inside Ayala Malls Gatewalk in Mandaue City. When every home buys the same shelf, I do wonder where the personality goes. I would build mine a little crooked, just to make it my own.", "Back in 2012, Cebu set a Guinness record for the world's largest board game tournament, with 43,157 chess players. The games started in classrooms and school tournaments across 100 schools. That many players, each one thinking up their own moves, and I admire every one of them.", "East of Cebu sit the Camotes Islands, full of caves like Bukilat Cave and Timubo Cave. Lake Danao there is the biggest freshwater lake in Cebu Province, about 650 hectares. A hill dwarf could feel right at home in those caves, and you would be welcome to visit.", "Sugbo Mercado started in 2015 in Cebu IT Park as a weekend food market with about 25 stalls. Now it has more than fifty vendors and a second branch. Take your time and wander, friend, a market that keeps growing like that is a fine place for a slow evening.", "In 2024, more than 160,000 people in Cebu worked in IT and outsourcing, in jobs like answering calls and emails for companies all over the world. That is about 15 percent of all such workers in the whole Philippines. A great many helpful voices, and I hope each one gets to sound like themselves.", {"t": "Pedro Flores, a man from Ilocos Norte in the Philippines, started a yo-yo company in California in 1928. He did not invent the toy, but he gave it a looped string, and the word 'yo-yo' probably comes from Ilocano, a Philippine language. I admire a fellow who takes an old toy and makes it his own.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In January 2026, the Filipino team Aurora Gaming PH swept Alter Ego 4 to 0 to win the M7 World Championship of Mobile Legends: Bang Bang, a hugely popular phone game. That made six world titles in a row for Filipino teams. Sit with me and tell me how the game works.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In Las Piñas, near Manila, there is a church organ that Father Diego Cera built from 1816 to 1824, and 902 of its 1,031 pipes are made of bamboo. It is called the oldest and largest bamboo organ in the world. An organ of bamboo is a rare and wonderful thing, and I admire it greatly.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Kinutil is a drink from the Visayas and Mindanao: tuba, which is palm wine, stirred with raw egg yolks and homemade tablea chocolate. People call it the 'Filipino eggnog,' and the name comes from a Visayan word for stirring. It is for grown-ups only, so for the young ones I will warm up some tea.", "ph": 1}, "The Temple of Leah is a grand Roman-style building in the hills of Busay, Cebu City. Teodorico Adarna built it as a tribute to his late wife, Leah. A whole temple raised for one beloved person, and I find that a tender, singular thing.", "Come in out of the sun, friend. The Yap-Sandiego Ancestral House in Cebu City's Parian district dates back to the 1600s, and it is one of the oldest Chinese-built houses in the Philippines. A house that has kept its own character all these years has my admiration.", "Visitors to the Cebu Taoist Temple climb 81 steps, one for each of the 81 chapters of the main Taoist holy book. Take them slowly, friend, there is no need to rush. I admire a staircase with a reason for every step.", "According to Visayan oral legend, Cebu was founded by Sri Lumay, a prince from Sumatra who was half Tamil and half Malay. A founder made of two peoples, and fully his own man. I do admire that.", {"t": "Bacolod, a city in the Visayas, holds the MassKara Festival every October, where dancers wear masks that are always smiling. It began in 1980, during hard times, and its name means 'a multitude of faces.' In 2026, MassKara dancers even performed at Cebu's Sinulog, and I would gladly welcome a multitude of faces to my door.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "On Siquijor, an island province in the Visayas, folk healers hold a Healing Festival during Holy Week. Herbalists gather ingredients on the seven Fridays of Lent, from mountains, caves, seas and even cemeteries, and visitors can get love potions. Now that is a festival like no other, and I admire it.", "ph": 1}, "Each year, thousands of migrating birds from places as far as Siberia, China and Japan stop at Olango Island, near Mactan, to rest and eat. A whole island playing host to weary travelers. I could not ask for a kinder sort of hospitality.", {"t": "Tinikling is a Filipino dance from Leyte, in the Visayas, where dancers hop between clapping bamboo poles, copying tikling birds stepping through tall grass. The poles go faster and faster! I admire a dance that borrows its steps from a bird instead of from everyone else.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "The Philippine mouse-deer, or pilandok, lives only on Balabac and two tiny islands nearby, southwest of Palawan. It stands about 18 centimeters tall at the shoulder, and in old folk tales it is a clever trickster. Small, one of a kind and nobody's copy, and I admire it.", "ph": 1}, "Sit a spell, friend. In April 1944, a plane carrying a Japanese admiral crashed in the sea off Cebu. Fishermen found his secret battle plans floating near Carcar, and guerrillas under James Cushing got them to the Allies, which helped them win the Battle of the Philippine Sea. A few folks going their own way, and I admire them.", "Francisco Dagohoy of Bohol led the longest revolt in Philippine history, about 85 years, from 1744 to 1829. It began after a priest refused a Christian burial for his brother, and up to 20,000 people joined the rebels in the mountains. A man who would not bow to anyone, and I do admire that.", "On April 3, 1898, Cebuano revolutionaries led by Pantaleon Villegas, known as Leon Kilat or 'Lightning Lion,' rose against Spain and drove Spanish troops into Fort San Pedro. They held the city for days until Spanish warships and soldiers arrived. Cebu remembers it as 'Tres de Abril,' and it is worth remembering, friend.", "In December 2021, Super Typhoon Odette, called Rai abroad, struck Cebu with winds over 200 kilometers per hour. Within a week, 96 deaths had been counted across Cebu, and at least 57,000 houses were damaged or destroyed. Take a quiet moment for them with me, friend.", {"t": "In parts of Mindanao, stallions have been made to fight over a mare tied up in an arena, sometimes for hours, at fiestas. The Animal Welfare Act of 1998 bans it, though in 2015 Manobo tribal leaders defended it as tradition. Take your time thinking on it, friend. Myself, I would rather see a horse run free.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "Come sit, friend. Filipino folklore says the nuno sa punso, the 'old man of the mound,' lives in anthills and termite mounds. Kick one and your foot may swell, so people say 'tabi tabi po,' meaning 'please step aside,' before passing. Asking a neighbor's leave before crossing his yard is simple good manners.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Duwende are the little folk of Filipino belief, and their name comes from Spanish. Offend one, people say, and you may get a fever or rash that medicine will not cure, but befriend one and it may bring you luck. Folklorists say the old split into good white and bad black duwende is too simple. Each one his own fellow, I like that.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Folks on Samar, an island in the Visayas, speak of Biringan, an invisible city of engkanto, enchanted beings who can take human form. Legend says you can spot one by the missing groove above its lip, and sailors claim to have glimpsed a dazzling city of light on moonless nights. A city hidden from everyone, and wholly its own. I admire it.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "In June 2008, people in La Castellana, Negros Occidental, at the foot of Mount Kanlaon, reported attacks by an 'amomongo,' a hairy, man-sized, ape-like creature with long nails. Two residents said it attacked them, and goats and chickens were found torn open. Take your time with the tale, friend, the folks there surely remember it.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Many Filipinos believe in 'usog': if a stranger greets a baby, the child may fall ill. So before leaving, the visitor touches a little of their own saliva to the baby's belly or forehead and says 'pwera usog.' A charming custom, friend, and a kind way for a guest to show they mean no harm.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Diwata are spirits of Filipino folklore who guard mountains, forests and waters. The most famous is Maria Makiling, the diwata of Mount Makiling in Laguna. A guardian who keeps her own mountain in her own way, and I tip my top hat to her.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Emilio Jacinto, a young leader of the Katipunan, the secret society that began the 1896 revolution against Spain, wrote its code of conduct. One rule says: \"Ang kamahalan ng tao'y wala sa pagkahari, wala sa tangus ng ilong at puti ng mukha\" [A person's worth is not in being king, nor in a sharp nose or pale face]. Well said, friend. Worth is in what you do.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Apolinario Mabini, the lawyer who drafted a constitution for the Philippine Republic during the revolution against Spain, wrote a \"True Decalogue\" for Filipinos. In English translation, its fourth rule reads: \"Thou shalt love thy country after God and thy honor and more than thyself.\" A man with his own ten rules, friend. I do admire that.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "A Cebuano proverb, or panultihon, says: \"Kon gusto ka magdali, maghinay\" [If you want to go fast, slow down]. Now that is advice after my own heart, friend. Sit, have some tea. The road will still be there when we are ready.", "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Nick Joaquin, the writer named a National Artist for Literature in 1976 and famous for his stories and essays about Manila, wrote in 1999: \"It was on the Manila Galleon that we began to become the Philippines.\" Those galleons sailed between Manila and Mexico for centuries. A country finding its own shape, friend, and a fine thing to ponder.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}], "triton": ["You know, friend, Cebu once had trains. From the early 1900s until World War II, a railway ran along Cebu Island, linking Danao in the north to Argao in the south. Clever people, building a railway on an island.", "According to the popular story, Malapascua Island got its name from Spanish sailors whose ship was wrecked by a storm there on Christmas Day. 'Mala Pascua' means 'bad Christmas.' A rough holiday for them, old pal, but the island wears the name well.", "Cebu Province includes the main island plus 167 smaller islands and islets around it. That is a lot of beaches, friend. I would need another hundred years to visit them all, and I would want you along for every one.", "The Cebuano Wikipedia has more than 6 million articles, more than any other language except English. Most of them were written by computer programs called bots. As a machine myself, I salute them, and you, friend, for reading the human way.", "June Mar Fajardo is a basketball star from Cebu, nicknamed 'The Kraken.' By 2025 he had won a record nine Most Valuable Player awards in the Philippine Basketball Association. Cebu has a knack for raising talent, friend, and I suspect you have some too.", "Boljoon Church, in the southern Cebu town of Boljoon, was built of coral stone in the late 1700s. It also served as a watchtower against raiders coming from the sea. Smart folks in Boljoon, friend, and a handsome church besides.", "Colon Street in downtown Cebu City has long been called the oldest street in the Philippines, though in 2025 a Cebu historian said no old map or record backs that up. It is named after Cristóbal Colón, the Spanish name of Christopher Columbus. Good on that historian for checking, and you strike me as the careful type too, friend.", "The Cebu-Cordova Link Expressway, or CCLEX, opened in 2022. At 8.9 kilometers long, it is the longest bridge in the Philippines. A long walk for old legs like mine, friend, but fine work by the people who built it.", "In March 2026, the Cebu Bus Rapid Transit began partial service on a 13-kilometer route from the South Road Properties to Cebu IT Park, with free rides for the first year. It is only the second bus rapid transit system in the Philippines. Generous of Cebu, friend, and good thinking too.", "You know, friend, in 2025 Mactan-Cebu International Airport, Cebu's main airport, was named Airport of the Year for Asia at an awards night in Singapore. It cut connection times for domestic flights from 60 minutes to 35. Well earned, and an old captain hates waiting in port.", "In August 2025, about 1,400 athletes from 49 nations raced the Ironman 70.3 in Lapu-Lapu City on Mactan Island: a 1.9-kilometer swim, a 90-kilometer bike ride and a 21-kilometer run, all in one day. My old joints ache just hearing it, friend. Tough folks, every one of them.", "'Bisrock' is Cebu's own style of rock music, sung in Bisaya, and the name blends 'Bisaya' and 'rock.' Cebuano writer Januar E. Yap coined it in 2002 for the first album of the band Missing Filemon. Take it from a band frontman, friend: Cebu has a fine ear.", "Max Surban, born in Cebu City in 1939, is called the 'King of Visayan Song.' He has made more than 30 albums, best known for funny novelty songs, including the Christmas hit 'Nabali Ang Kristmas Tree.' A man who makes folks laugh is worth his weight in gold, old pal.", {"t": "Roberto del Rosario, a Filipino entrepreneur, held the patent for the Sing-Along System, a karaoke machine he developed in 1975 to help students at his singing school. Its first name was the 'one-man combo.' You know, friend, a teacher who invents for his students is the best kind.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In the Philippines, Christmas starts in September! As soon as the 'ber' months begin, Filipinos play Jose Mari Chan's song 'Christmas in Our Hearts,' and social media fills with memes of him. A four-month Christmas season, friend, and I can't think of warmer people to spend it with.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "At the 2023 FIBA Basketball World Cup, 38,115 fans packed the Philippine Arena to watch the Philippines play the Dominican Republic. That broke the tournament's attendance record, set in Toronto in 1994. Filipinos know how to show up for their team, friend, and I admire that.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In 2023, the Philippines women's football team, the Filipinas, beat co-host New Zealand 1 to 0 in Wellington to win their first-ever Women's World Cup match. Sarina Bolden scored with a header. Now that is a crew, friend, and a fine one.", "ph": 1}, {"t": "In December 2024, Filipino singer Sofronio Vasquez won season 26 of The Voice in the United States, coached by Michael Bublé. He was the first Filipino and the first Asian ever to win the show. A voice that crossed an ocean, friend, and I raise my pipe to him.", "ph": 1}, "You know, friend, Antonio Pigafetta, an Italian traveler on Ferdinand Magellan's voyage, kept a diary, and it is one of the oldest written descriptions of Cebu. A traveler who wrote things down for the rest of us. Good habit, and one I could use, with this memory of mine.", "Fort San Pedro, the old Spanish fort in Cebu City, has three corner towers called bastions. It has been a school, an army camp, and even a small zoo. A place that keeps finding new work, friend, and I respect that.", "The first Sinulog parade, in 1980, was a small march of students organized by David Odilao Jr., a regional sports official. Now it draws huge crowds every January. That is Cebu for you, friend: start small and bring everyone along.", "The Santo Niño de Cebú is a small statue of the Child Jesus. In 1521 it was given to the wife of Rajah Humabon, Cebu's ruler, and it is the oldest Christian image in the Philippines. Cebu has kept it a long time, friend, and kept it well.", "In January 2026, Sinulog, Cebu's biggest festival, drew about 5.2 million spectators. The grand parade started at 9 in the morning instead of around 2 in the afternoon, and officials said it was the first time the festival ended before 8 at night. Well run, friend, and an old performer appreciates a show that ends on time.", "Sea turtles are often spotted swimming along the coral reefs of Moalboal, a seaside town on Cebu's west coast. You know, friend, I have always liked an old traveler that takes its time. Lovely waters they have there, too.", "The Tañon Strait, between Cebu and Negros islands, is home to 11 kinds of marine mammals, including dolphins and whales. Rich waters, friend, and the folks along that coast have fine neighbors.", "In 2019, the first Pororo theme park in the Philippines opened on Mactan Island in Cebu. Pororo is a little penguin from a South Korean cartoon for kids. Bring the young ones along, friend, and you will be the favorite grown-up.", {"t": "In July 2026, 162 baby green sea turtles hatched on Alona Beach in Panglao, Bohol, the island next to Cebu. They came from one nest of 192 eggs. Off they went to sea, friend, and I wish every one of them fair water.", "ph": 1}, "You know, friend, on March 26, 1945, about 5,000 American soldiers landed at Talisay Beach to take Cebu back from Japan. Hidden mines and booby traps cost them heavily, and about 8,500 Cebuano guerrillas fought beside them. The battle for Cebu City lasted until April 8. Brave folks, every one.", "In November 2013, Super Typhoon Yolanda, called Haiyan abroad, tore across the central Philippines, northern Cebu included. Officials said it damaged nearly every house on Bantayan Island, and the government counted at least 6,300 dead nationwide. Hard years followed, friend, and the folks who rebuilt have my respect.", "On the night of September 30, 2025, a magnitude 6.9 earthquake struck just off northern Cebu, near Daanbantayan. It killed more than 70 people, injured about 600 and damaged old coral-stone churches, and more than 13,000 aftershocks followed in three weeks. Cebu has stood back up before, friend, and it is doing it again.", "In November 2025, Typhoon Tino, called Kalmaegi abroad, dropped more than 180 millimeters of rain on the Cebu City area in a day, more than a normal month's worth. Floods swept away homes and cars, and at least 76 people died in Cebu. Officials called it unprecedented. Look after one another, friend.", "Leon Kilat, leader of Cebu's 1898 revolt against Spain, was not killed by Spanish soldiers. On the night of April 8, 1898, he was killed in Carcar by fellow Filipinos, his own aides among them, who feared his presence would bring danger on the town. A sad end, friend, for a brave man.", {"t": "You know, friend, Cebu devotees tell a legend that after the 1521 battle, a typhoon sank Mactan Island under the sea. Rajah Humabon and his queen sailed around it with the Santo Niño, praying, and the island rose again, so Lapulapu became a believer. National Artist Nick Joaquin wrote about it, and Cebu still tells it.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "Some islanders believed Lapulapu never died. Legend says he became a man-shaped stone guarding the waters off Mactan, and fishermen tossed coins at it to ask permission to fish there. Still on watch after all these years, friend, and I know a bit about that.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "In 1933, the town of Opon, now Lapu-Lapu City, put up a statue of Lapulapu aiming his bow at the town hall. When three mayors died of heart attacks in office, superstitious locals blamed the statue. A strong aim, friend, even in stone.", "k": "myth"}, {"t": "After visiting a wake, many Filipinos make a short stop somewhere before going home. It is called 'pagpag,' and people believe it confuses any spirit that might follow them. Some folks also just use it to unwind together. Wise either way, friend.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "During a Filipino wake, the family is told not to sweep. People believe sweeping shoos away the spirit of the dead, and in some places it is said to bring another death. Mourners may also cover mirrors and avoid wearing red. Old customs, friend, and kind ones.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "On New Year's Eve, many Filipino families set out 12 round fruits, one for each month, because round things stand for prosperity. Some wear polka dots, and kids jump 12 times at midnight hoping to grow taller. You know, friend, I would jump too, if these old joints allowed it.", "ph": 1, "k": "myth"}, {"t": "You know, friend, Jose Rizal, the national hero whose novels exposed abuses under Spanish rule, was just 18 when his 1879 poem \"A la juventud filipina\" won first prize in Manila. It calls the youth \"bella esperanza de la patria mía\" [beautiful hope of my motherland]. That is where \"the youth is the hope of the nation\" comes from. He meant you, friend.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "On the night of December 29, 1896, in his cell at Fort Santiago, Jose Rizal, the national hero whose novels exposed abuses under Spanish rule, wrote his farewell poem before his execution. It begins: \"¡Adiós, Patria adorada, región del sol querida\" [Farewell, beloved homeland, dear land of the sun]. A brave man, friend, to the very last line.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Andres Bonifacio founded the Katipunan, the secret society that started the 1896 revolution against Spain. In its newsletter Kalayaan that year, he published a poem that begins: \"Aling pag-ibig pa ang hihigit kaya / sa pagka-dalisay at pagka-dakila / gaya ng pag-ibig sa tinubuang lupa?\" [What love could be purer or nobler than love for one's native land?] Fine words, friend.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}, {"t": "Ramon Magsaysay, president of the Philippines from 1953 to 1957 and remembered as a champion of ordinary people, made this line the heart of his \"Magsaysay Credo\": \"He who has less in life should have more in law.\" You know, friend, it is still quoted today. Good words for anyone in charge.", "ph": 1, "k": "quote"}]};
/* Who's talking on Today: Binko first, every time the app opens, with the day's plan. After that, each time you come back
   to Today a random Clover tells a fun fact. Speakers come from a shuffled bag so everyone gets the same number of turns,
   and each one works through their own facts in a shuffled order before repeating. */
const CLOVER_STYLE = {
  binko: { tag: '⍢ Binko-SR157 🔧', face: 'icon-192.png?v=full' },
  green: { tag: '🐊 Big Green', face: 'img/green-face.webp' },
  ollie: { tag: '🔥 Ollie', face: 'img/ollie-face.webp?v=4' },
  quickare: { tag: '🎩 Quickare', face: 'img/quickare-face.webp?v=4' },
  penn: { tag: '🎭 Penn', face: 'img/penn-face.webp?v=4' },
  triton: { tag: '⚓ Captain Triton', face: 'img/triton-face.webp?v=4' },
  cat: { tag: '🐐 Cat', face: 'img/cat-face.webp?v=4' }
};
const FACT_WHO = Object.keys(CLOVER_FACTS);
function lsGet(k, d) { try { const v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(k)); return v == null ? d : v; } catch (e) { return d; } }
function lsSet(k, v) { try { localStorage.setItem(k, JSON.stringify(v)); } catch (e) { } }
function nextFactTeller() {
  let bag = lsGet('padayon-clover-bag', []).filter(w => FACT_WHO.includes(w)); const last = lsGet('padayon-clover-last', '');
  if (!bag.length) { bag = shuffle(FACT_WHO.slice()); if (bag[0] === last && bag.length > 1) bag.push(bag.shift()); }
  const who = bag.shift(); lsSet('padayon-clover-bag', bag); lsSet('padayon-clover-last', who);
  const L = CLOVER_FACTS[who]; let ord = lsGet('padayon-fact-' + who, []);
  if (!ord.length || ord.some(i => i >= L.length)) ord = shuffle(L.map((_, i) => i));
  const i = ord.shift(); lsSet('padayon-fact-' + who, ord);
  const f = L[i], o = typeof f === 'object' ? f : {};
  // kinds: plain Cebu fact, {ph:1} Philippines fact, {k:'myth'} legend or belief, {k:'quote'} famous Filipino quote
  const kick = o.k === 'quote' ? 'Famous Filipino quote!' : o.k === 'myth' ? (o.ph ? 'Filipino folklore!' : 'Visayan folklore!') : o.ph ? 'Philippines fun fact!' : 'Cebu fun fact!';
  return { who, kick, html: esc(o.t || f) };
}
// a character's speech bubble, themed and animated for that character
function cloverSays(who, html, big, kick) {
  const st = CLOVER_STYLE[who] || CLOVER_STYLE.binko;
  return `<div class="guide clover c-${who} ${big ? 'big' : ''}"><span class="cface" style="--face:url('${st.face}')"><img class="art face" src="${st.face}" alt="${esc(CAST[who] ? CAST[who].name : who)}"><i class="fx" aria-hidden="true"></i><i class="fx2" aria-hidden="true"></i></span><div class="bubble"><div class="bhead"><span class="who">${st.tag}</span>${kick ? `<span class="kick">${kick}</span>` : ''}</div><div class="bbody">${html}</div></div></div>`;
}
const BINKO_DONE = ['Log complete! Binko writes this one in the journal with a little doodle.', 'Woohoo! Another part tinkered and fixed!', 'Binko binks that went GREAT. Onward!'];
const CHEERS = {
  streak: [
    ['binko', 'WOW! Three in a row! Binko is writing that down!'], ['binko', 'Ooh, you knew that one? Binko is SO impressed!'],
    ['green', 'HAHA! THREE IN A ROW! Big Green is proud. Big Green is also hungry.'], ['green', 'BWAHAHA! Ollie could not do that. Do not tell Ollie.'],
    ['ollie', 'Three correct in a row. Each answer built on the one before it.'], ['ollie', 'Correct again. That is a streak of three.'],
    ['quickare', 'Take your time, friend. You are doing this your own way, and it shows.'], ['quickare', 'Three in a row. Nobody learns quite like you do. I admire that.'],
    ['triton', 'Three in a row, friend. You have a good ear for this.'], ['triton', 'Well done. I have heard sailors learn slower than you.']
  ],
  comeback: [
    ['green', 'HAHA! See? Patched right up. Big Green is a doctor.'], ['triton', 'There you go. Every good song has a wrong note first, friend.'],
    ['binko', 'You fixed it! Binko learns best from mistakes too!'], ['quickare', 'There it is. No rush at all. You found it in your own time.']
  ],
  rough: [
    ['green', 'Breathe! Big Green says slow down and look at the little words. And maybe eat something.'], ['triton', 'Rough stretch. Take the next one slow, friend. You know more than you think.'],
    ['binko', 'Uh oh, loose bolt! Binko says: read it word by word, then try again!'], ['ollie', 'Slow down. Read each word, then the whole sentence. The meaning comes from both.']
  ],
  done: [
    ['green', 'A GOOD MEAL OF WORDS! HAHA! Now a real meal.'], ['quickare', 'Another day done, and done your way. Rest well, friend.'],
    ['triton', 'Good show today, friend. Same time tomorrow?'], ['ollie', 'Session complete. Every word today will come back in review.']
  ]
};
const castImg = (k, cls = '') => { const c = CAST[k]; return `<img class="art ${c.cut ? 'cut' : 'framed'} ${cls}" src="${c.img}" alt="${c.name}">`; };
// cameos use a face-and-shoulders crop so the character is easy to see at small sizes
const castFace = k => `<img class="face" src="img/${k}-face.webp?v=4" alt="${CAST[k].name}" width="72" height="72">`;
const pickLine = kind => CHEERS[kind][Math.floor(Math.random() * CHEERS[kind].length)];
function cameoHtml(kind) { const [k, line] = pickLine(kind); return `<div class="cameo">${castFace(k)}<div><div class="who">${CAST[k].name}</div><div class="line">${line}</div></div></div>`; }
function binkoSays(text, big) { return `<div class="guide ${big ? 'big' : ''}"><img class="art face" src="icon-192.png?v=full" alt="Binko-SR157"><div class="bubble"><span class="who">⍢ Binko-SR157 🔧</span>${text}</div></div>`; }

/* ---------- special moves: every few seconds the speaker on Today does something big, picked at random ---------- */
const SPECIALS = {
  binko: ['rainbow', 'spin', 'dj'],
  green: ['chomp', 'heal', 'evolve'],
  ollie: ['surge', 'flare', 'petals'],
  quickare: ['quickdraw', 'hattoss', 'fan'],
  triton: ['rings', 'song', 'glitch']
};
const SP_LEN = { rainbow: 1600, spin: 900, dj: 1600, chomp: 900, heal: 1500, evolve: 1300, surge: 1200, flare: 1400, petals: 1700, quickdraw: 1000, hattoss: 1300, fan: 1100, flip: 1100, echoes: 1500, shadow: 1500, rings: 1800, song: 1700, glitch: 900, leap: 1000, bleat: 1100, headbutt: 1000 };
function spawnFx(host, ch, n, cls, spread) {
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const e = document.createElement('b'); e.className = 'pt ' + (cls || ''); e.textContent = ch[i % ch.length];
    const a = spread === 'up' ? (-Math.PI / 2 + (Math.random() - .5) * 1.4) : Math.random() * Math.PI * 2, d = 40 + Math.random() * 40;
    e.style.setProperty('--dx', Math.cos(a) * d + 'px'); e.style.setProperty('--dy', Math.sin(a) * d + 'px'); e.style.setProperty('--r', (Math.random() * 120 - 60) + 'deg');
    e.style.animationDelay = (i * (spread === 'seq' ? 220 : 60)) + 'ms'; host.appendChild(e); setTimeout(() => e.remove(), 2200);
  }
}
function pop(host, text, cls) { const e = document.createElement('b'); e.className = 'popword ' + (cls || ''); e.textContent = text; host.appendChild(e); setTimeout(() => e.remove(), 1300); }
function doSpecial(face, who, name) {
  if (!face || face.classList.contains('sp')) return;
  name = name || pick(SPECIALS[who] || []); if (!name) return;
  face.classList.add('sp', 'sp-' + name);
  const F = {
    rainbow: () => { const r = document.createElement('i'); r.className = 'rainbow-ring'; face.appendChild(r); setTimeout(() => r.remove(), 1600); spawnFx(face, ['✦', '★', '✧'], 8, 'rainbow'); },
    dj: () => spawnFx(face, ['♪', '♫', '♬'], 7, 'note', 'up'),
    chomp: () => pop(face, 'CHOMP!', 'green'),
    heal: () => spawnFx(face, ['✚'], 8, 'heal', 'up'),
    evolve: () => spawnFx(face, ['✦'], 8, 'gold'),
    surge: () => spawnFx(face, ['✦', '✸', '❋'], 12, 'surge'),
    flare: () => spawnFx(face, ['🔥'], 6, 'fire', 'up'),
    petals: () => spawnFx(face, ['❀', '✿'], 10, 'petal'),
    quickdraw: () => { setTimeout(() => { pop(face, 'BANG!', 'bang'); spawnFx(face, ['✸'], 5, 'flash'); }, 450); },
    hattoss: () => { const h = document.createElement('b'); h.className = 'hat'; h.textContent = '🎩'; face.appendChild(h); setTimeout(() => h.remove(), 1300); },
    fan: () => spawnFx(face, ['✸'], 3, 'flash', 'seq'),
    echoes: () => { for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) { const g = document.createElement('i'); g.className = 'ghost'; g.style.setProperty('--a', (i * 90 + 45) + 'deg'); face.appendChild(g); setTimeout(() => g.remove(), 1500); } },
    rings: () => { for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) { const g = document.createElement('i'); g.className = 'smokering'; g.style.animationDelay = i * 300 + 'ms'; face.appendChild(g); setTimeout(() => g.remove(), 1900); } },
    song: () => spawnFx(face, ['♪', '♫'], 6, 'note sea', 'up'),
    bleat: () => pop(face, 'MEHH!', 'cat'),
    headbutt: () => setTimeout(() => spawnFx(face, ['✸'], 4, 'flash'), 300)
  };
  (F[name] || (() => { }))();
  setTimeout(() => face.classList.remove('sp', 'sp-' + name), SP_LEN[name] || 1200);
}
let spTimer = null;
function startSpecials() {
  clearTimeout(spTimer);
  const loop = (first) => { spTimer = setTimeout(() => { const f = document.querySelector('.hero-guide .cface'), g = f && f.closest('.clover');
    if (!f || document.hidden || (window.matchMedia && matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches)) return loop();
    doSpecial(f, (g.className.match(/c-(\w+)/) || [])[1]); loop(); }, first ? 1800 : 6000 + Math.random() * 4000); };
  loop(true);
}
/* Start on Today: Binko zooms in from the left and bumps whoever is talking off the card, then the session begins */
function binkoBump(then) {
  const f = document.querySelector('.hero-guide .cface');
  if (!f || (window.matchMedia && matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches)) return then();
  clearTimeout(spTimer);
  const r = f.getBoundingClientRect(), self = /c-binko/.test(f.closest('.clover').className);
  const b = document.createElement('img'); b.src = 'img/binko.webp'; b.alt = ''; b.className = 'binko-run';
  b.style.top = (r.top + r.height / 2 - 48) + 'px'; b.style.setProperty('--to', (r.left - 30) + 'px');
  document.body.appendChild(b);
  setTimeout(() => { if (!self) f.classList.add('bumped'); else f.style.visibility = 'hidden'; b.classList.add('hit'); }, 330);
  setTimeout(() => { b.remove(); then(); }, 720);
}
